window.docContents[108].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics","header":"8.7. The Three-Dimensional Regularity Problem","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--Integration-by-parts-and-weak-derivatives":{"contents":"Let , , and\n be a Schwartz test function.  Thenwhere  and  are interpreted through their physical\n representatives.  A second theorem specializes this identity to the\nproject's compactly supported real test functions and coordinate derivative\npartialDeriv, exactly matching the sign convention used by\nHasWeakDerivativeOn.\n\nPair the tempered distribution  against the test function.\nBy definition of distributional differentiation this is the negative pairing\nof  against .  Replace both pairings by integrals of\nthe physical  representatives.  Complexifying a compactly supported real\ntest function gives the coordinate form.\n\nAfter taking real parts, the integration-by-parts identity is the defining\nequation of HasWeakDerivativeOn. The realness theorems of the previous\nsection guarantee that nothing is lost in taking real parts when the physical\nrepresentative of  is real, since directional differentiation preserves\nrealness.\n\nSuppose  and the physical representative of  is real.\nFor every coordinate , its real part has the weak derivative represented\nby the real part of the physical representative of :in the HasWeakDerivativeOn sense on all of .\n\nThe integration-by-parts identity gives the required test-function equation.\nDirectional differentiation preserves real-valuedness, so both complex\n representatives are almost everywhere complexifications of their real\nparts.  Taking real parts converts the complex identity into the real weak\nderivative relation.\n\nEach application of the first-order theorem lowers the order by one and\npreserves realness of the physical representative, so it can be applied again\nas long as the current order is at least one. The condition \nis exactly what keeps every intermediate order in range.\n\nLet  be a word of coordinate directions with\n.  If  has a real physical\nrepresentative, then there is a real-represented element\n whose physical representative is the weak\n-derivative of that of .In Lean the target order is an existential  together with the equation\n, which avoids rewriting along casts of real\nnumbers.\n\nInduct on the derivative word from the right.  The empty word uses the\nfunction itself.  At each successor step, apply the first-order theorem in the\ncurrent Sobolev order, appending one coordinate direction to the derivative word.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Fourier Sobolev Spaces\u0009Fourier and Fractional Sobolev Spaces","header":"4.5.1.4. Integration by parts and weak derivatives","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--Integration-by-parts-and-weak-derivatives"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Certified-Numerical-Enclosures":{"contents":"The numerical command\n\n-- Enclose every value on the interval,\n-- rather than sampling points.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas [enclose x in [-1 / 10, 1 / 10]] (cos x)\n\n\nreturns\n\n#cas [enclose x in [-1 / 10, 1 / 10]]\nDomain: x ∈ [-1/10, 1/10] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (cos x)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [0.995004, 1]\nUpper bound: 1\nAbsolute bound: 1\nCenter and radius: ≈0.997502 ± 0.002498\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe output bounds every value of cosine throughout the input interval. The\ndecimal preview rounds outward; the certificate retains exact rational\nendpoints. It also implies the coarser bound\n\n\n\n\n\nThe figure uses a rational function to show why a valid enclosure can still\nbe loose. Interval arithmetic treats repeated occurrences of the variable\nindependently, losing the relation between numerator and denominator.\nSubdivision restricts that loss on each smaller panel. The cosine query\nhas the same obligation: its bounds must contain every value between\nthe endpoints, however the evaluator finds them. The figure illustrates the\nmechanism; the displayed theorem is the guarantee for the cosine calculation.\n\nFor a concrete example of that dependence loss, consider\n on . Write , so .\nBounding numerator and denominator separately gives .\nTo obtain the sharper upper bound, retain their common . Since\n,\n\n\n\nTogether with nonnegativity this proves , with equality\nat . A valid interval can be wider than the actual range; a\nfailed request for a tighter interval need not mean that the requested bound\nis false. It may require a different representation or subdivision.\n\nReturning to cosine, the coarser enclosure can be stated as an ordinary theorem\nabout the one-dimensional Euclidean space and closed by cega:\n\nopen CAS.Certificate in\nexample :\n    EnclosedOn (fun p : ℝ^1 ↦ Real.cos (p 0))\n      {p | -1/10 ≤ p 0 ∧ p 0 ≤ 1/10} (716402999/720000000) 1 := by\n  cega [enclose x in [-1/10, 1/10]] (Real.cos x)\n\n\nThe rational interval computation supplies the candidate endpoints. The\ntheorem-facing finite-box API exposes the same guarantee through\nCAS.CEGA.encloseBox? and the returned attempt's\nEnclosureAttempt.sound; the more general encloseOn? API accepts\nan arbitrary semantic domain with a proved interval environment. A theorem can\nlater consume the resulting certificate without knowing which interval\nalgorithm produced it.\n\n-- Import the certificate vocabulary used\n-- by the theorem statement.\nopen CAS.Certificate\n\n-- CEGA exposes the certificate's stored soundness theorem.\nexample\n    (domain : Set ℝ)\n    (certificate : EnclosureCert ℝ domain) :\n    EnclosedOn certificate.fn domain\n      certificate.lo certificate.hi := by\n  cega using certificate\n\n\nThose endpoints are exact rationals. The proposition bounds a real-valued\nfunction, while the endpoint arithmetic uses rational numbers. The certificate\nconnects that arithmetic to the function. Endpoint representations and\nchecking costs are discussed in the Zulip threads\nInterval arithmetic -- what approach?\nand\nLeanCert.\nHere subdivision and an explicit precision option control how much work the\nenclosure calculation performs.\n\nThe same box computation also proves the other numerical claims. Below the\ncertified interval the report gives an upper bound, a bound on the magnitude,\nand the midpoint of the interval with its radius as a uniform error:\n\n-- One certified interval and the bounds\n-- read off it.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas [enclose x in [0, 1]] (x - 2)\n\n\n#cas [enclose x in [0, 1]]\nDomain: x ∈ [0, 1] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (x - 2)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [-2, -1]\nUpper bound: -1\nAbsolute bound: 2\nCenter and radius: -3/2 ± 1/2\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.6. Certified Numerical Enclosures","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Certified-Numerical-Enclosures"}});